MOUNT_ISOFS(8)       UNIX Programmer's Manual       MOUNT_ISOFS(8)



NAME
     mount_isofs - mount an ISO 9660 file system

SYNOPSIS
     mount_isofs [ -o options ] special node

DESCRIPTION
     Mount_isofs attaches the ISO 9660 file system on the block
     device special -- a CD-ROM, /dev/cd0, or an image on a disk
     -- to the directory node.  It is not normally run by hand:
     mount(8) runs it for "mount -t isofs".  The options are
     mount(8)'s -o list.

     The file system is always mounted read-only.  A device that
     is not an ISO 9660 volume is reported as "not an ISO 9660
     volume".

     The file system is the module isofs, which is not loaded at
     boot; mount_isofs loads it with modload(8) the first time it
     is needed.

NAMES
     An ISO 9660 name is listed in lowercase, without its
     ";version" suffix or the "." before one: README.TXT;1 is
     readme.txt.  Lookup ignores case, and a ";version" may be
     given.  An associated file, which a Macintosh disc uses for
     a resource fork, is listed as =name.

ROCK RIDGE
     A volume with the Rock Ridge extensions (RRIP 1.09 or 1.12)
     is read through them: names keep their case and their
     length, files have their modes, owners, groups and link
     counts, and symbolic links work.  A directory Rock Ridge
     moved under rr_moved to get past ISO 9660's eight levels is
     found where it belongs, and rr_moved itself is listed too.

     A Rock Ridge name longer than 63 characters, the longest
     this system can hold, is listed under its ISO 9660 name
     instead.

EXAMPLES
     Mount the CD in the first CD-ROM drive on /mnt:

          mount_isofs /dev/cd0 /mnt

SEE ALSO
     mount(8), umount(8), mount_prodos(8), modload(8)

HISTORY
     The file system and this command appeared in NetBSD 0.9.
     The Rock Ridge support is 4.4BSD-Lite2's.

BUGS
     Joliet names, which discs made on Windows and macOS carry,
     are not read; such a disc lists its ISO 9660 names.

     Only a disc's first session is read.

     A file recorded interleaved is refused with EIO.

     A file's inode number is folded to 16 bits and can repeat on
     a large volume.

     Owner, group and mode cannot be chosen at mount time.



